phase_section='''<h2>Phased investing after a crash-entry inheritance</h2><p>Test a lump sum parked in a short Treasury ladder/rolling maturities spanning 3 months to 2 years for 24, 30 or 36 months, then invested in stocks over 12 months. The table compares that path with investing the same illustrative proceeds in equities immediately. It is an educational sequence-risk experiment, not the household forecast or a historical replay.</p>'''+table('SYNTHETIC annual equity paths · assumptions only',['Stress path','Assumed stock returns by year 1–10','Status / limitation','Official context'],phase_assumptions,'illustration')+table('SYNTHETIC ending value · $100,000 illustrative starting amount · 10-year horizon',['Stress path','Immediate equity entry','Treasury wait then 12-month phase','Phased terminal value','Phased minus immediate','Official context'],phase_results,'illustration')+table('WHAT-IF · fast crash/recovery path · 30-month wait · 12-month phase',['Assumed rolling Treasury return','Phased terminal value','Difference vs immediate equity','Entry schedule','Official context'],phase_yield_rows,'illustration')+notice('Reinvestment risk beside the headline','The parking return is a flat assumed 3% per year, not a Treasury quote or yield forecast; the what-if table varies it from 2% to 4%. Actual 3-month-to-2-year yields and maturity-date reinvestment rates can differ. Staging benefits these chosen early-crash paths but lags immediate equity entry in the rising-market path; it does not remove market loss, assure a higher return, or make a 24–36 month wait optimal. The official links give historical / diversification context only and do not validate the synthetic returns. For the same question tested on the real annual record, with the cash leg taken from official Federal Reserve series, see <a href="phased.html">Staged entry after a crash</a>; that page also shows that in the median historical entry year staging ended behind a lump sum.')
